  "slug": "best-of-bristol-2015-theatre",
  "title": "Best of Bristol 2015: Theatre",
  "content": "<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/the-fix-theatre-637x450.jpg\" alt=\"Image: Richard Davenport\" width=\"637\" height=\"450\" class=\"aligncenter wp-image-10659 size-large\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/the-fix-theatre-637x450.jpg 637w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/the-fix-theatre-300x212.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/the-fix-theatre.jpg 842w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 637px) 100vw, 637px\" /></p>\n<p>In the second of our retrospectives of 2015, we run down what we thought was the best <strong>Theatre</strong> in Bristol over the last twelve months, and choose our number one.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/Theatre-Ad-Infinitum-Light-c-Alex-Brenner2-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignleft size-thumbnail wp-image-6082\" />For me, 2015 was a triumph for smaller-scale theatre productions. Away from the blockbusters were some superb, finely-tuned treats on offer in venues across Bristol. Theatre Ad Infinitum’s mime piece </span><b><i><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/review-light-bristol-old-vic/\" target=\"_blank\">Light</a></i></b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> brought a dystopian vision of the future to the Bristol Old Vic Studio in what was one of the most terrifying and affecting shows that I have ever seen. <br /></span></p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/70646cd7d2a074103949cd265cfe541c-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"REVIEW: Grounded, Arnolfini\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignright wp-image-5693 size-thumbnail\" /></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">George Brant’s </span><b><i><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/review-grounded-arnolfini/\">Grounded</a></i></b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, meanwhile, showcased a mesmerising one-woman performance at the Arnolfini as Lucy Ellinson took on the role of a drone pilot &#8211; isolated in her remote control military role, as well as in her on-stage cube. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/05/ORARH-new2-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"ORARH-new2\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignleft size-thumbnail wp-image-7957\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/05/ORARH-new2-150x150.jpg 150w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/05/ORARH-new2-45x45.jpg 45w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 150px) 100vw, 150px\" />For me, though, the most memorable show this year came courtesy of Still House’s </span><b><i><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/mayfest-of-riders-and-running-horses-is-one-of-the-most-radiant-shows-you-will-ever-see/\" target=\"_blank\">Of Riders and Running Horses</a></i></b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, part of the excellent Mayfest festival. Staged on top of the NCP car park, the sextet of dancers &#8211; accompanied by a live Typesun soundtrack &#8211; created an atmosphere of unbridled energy and joy. This was made all the more dramatic by the bristling weather, which battered the audience with wind and hail. That the performance ended with a radiant mass dance by the audience is a testament to the effervescent production. <strong>(CD)</strong></span></p>\n<hr />\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Bustle-2-150x150.jpg\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-8672 size-thumbnail\" height=\"150\" width=\"150\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Bustle-2-150x150.jpg 150w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Bustle-2-45x45.jpg 45w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 150px) 100vw, 150px\" /></p>\n<p>Telling the story of Alan Turing, Gordon Welchman and the code cracking heroes of Bletchley Park, Idle Motion’s spellbinding <em><b><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/that-is-all-you-need-to-know/\" target=\"_blank\">That Is All You Need To Know</a></b></em> at Tobacco Factory was an innovative tribute to the relatively unsung heroes of the Second World War. Aside from its stylish storytelling, this was a play set apart by its ingenious presentation. Highly effective use of multimedia – video projections on to handkerchiefs, authentic audio testimony and slick visual displays –served to make this a touching, humorous and educational piece of theatre.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/The-Crucible-full-company-Bristol-Old-Vic-Production-Pics-Photo-by-Geraint-Lewis-48-LOW-RES-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"The Crucible at Bristol Old Vic\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignright wp-image-9965 size-thumbnail\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/The-Crucible-full-company-Bristol-Old-Vic-Production-Pics-Photo-by-Geraint-Lewis-48-LOW-RES-150x150.jpg 150w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/The-Crucible-full-company-Bristol-Old-Vic-Production-Pics-Photo-by-Geraint-Lewis-48-LOW-RES-45x45.jpg 45w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 150px) 100vw, 150px\" /></p>\n<p>Any rendering of American drama staple <b><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/review-bristol-old-vic-delivers-intense-and-brooding-rendering-of-the-crucible/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>The Crucible</em></a> </b>will rise or fall based on its ability to capture the intensity and foreboding doom of Arthur Miller’s allegory for McCarthyism – this accomplished Bristol Old Vic production delivered just that. Set during the Salem witch trials of the 1690s and inspired by Senator Joe McCarthy’s anti-communist oppression of the 1950s, Miller’s play is a great example of art that has retained thematic relevance. A dynamic interpretation &#8211; thanks, in no small part, to the powerful performance of Dean Lennox-Kelly as protagonist John Proctor. <strong>(SH)<br /></strong></p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"The Crucible Trailer\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/phqBwJmz3xc?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/69157b0ea2165ad202fffa7fe8e6adfe-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"REVIEW: Romeo &amp; Juliet, Tobacco Factory\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-6263 size-thumbnail\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/69157b0ea2165ad202fffa7fe8e6adfe-150x150.jpg 150w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/69157b0ea2165ad202fffa7fe8e6adfe-45x45.jpg 45w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 150px) 100vw, 150px\" /></p>\n<p>Tobacco Factory&#8217;s powerful and zestful run of <em><strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/review-romeo-juliet-tobacco-factory/\" target=\"_blank\">Romeo &amp; Juliet</a></strong></em> was a<span>s remarkable as it was memorable. The vivid and unforgettable adaptation of the star crossed lovers was a thing of brutal beauty.</span> Paapa Essiedu starred as the charmingly funny, devastatingly good looking and fearfully believable bi-polar Romeo, achieving the admirable feat of both tender adonis and wild murderer with total conviction. But it was Oliver Hoare’s Mercurio, who was as mad as he was enrapturing who stole the show with a breathless performance that took the rakish Prince of fair Verona to the unnerving and uncouth Johnny Rotten and wilder side of Daniel Day-Lewis. </p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/stick_house_image_flying-150x150.jpg\" alt=\"stick_house_image_flying\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"alignright size-thumbnail wp-image-9727\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/stick_house_image_flying-150x150.jpg 150w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/stick_house_image_flying-45x45.jpg 45w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 150px) 100vw, 150px\" />Extending the very boundaries of theatre, Raucous Theatre&#8217;s fantastical <em><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/review-raucous-push-the-boundaries-of-theatre-with-the-stick-house/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>The Stick House</strong></a></em> was simply breathtaking in it&#8217;s all round brilliance. A promenade performance in a masterful set in the caverns below Temple Meads train station, this<span> gothic tale reeked of rust and decay, and had a real sense of modernity to it given the 21</span><sup>st</sup><span> century touches throughout. </span>Its ambition, its execution, its passion, it’s variety; this really was as good as theatre gets. <strong>(KM)</strong></p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"The Stick House Trailer (Feb 2015) v1\" src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/119874779?dnt=1&amp;app_id=122963\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\"></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/And-the-winner-is-300x263.png\" alt=\"And the winner is\" width=\"300\" height=\"263\" class=\"aligncenter size-medium wp-image-10753\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/And-the-winner-is-300x263.png 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/And-the-winner-is-768x673.png 768w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/And-the-winner-is-514x450.png 514w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/And-the-winner-is.png 1972w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Of-running.png\" alt=\"Of running\" width=\"1524\" height=\"525\" class=\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-10785\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Of-running.png 1524w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Of-running-300x103.png 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Of-running-768x265.png 768w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Of-running-978x337.png 978w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1524px) 100vw, 1524px\" /></p>\n<p>Mayfest stars again with this sumptuous performance, blending dance, live music and a stunning outdoor location.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Contributions by Kevin McGough (KM), Conal Dougan (CD) and Scott Hammond (SH).</strong></p>\n<p></p>",
